1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,412
How Do Touchscreens Work?

2
00:00:02,362 --> 00:00:06,875
Modern smartphones predominantly employ capacitive touchscreens.

3
00:00:06,875 --> 00:00:15,100
The glass panel is coated with indium tin oxide (ITO), a transparent conductor that stores a uniform electrostatic field.

4
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:23,687
The human body is electrically conductive; when a finger contacts the screen, it draws a minute alternating current from each corner of the panel.

5
00:00:23,687 --> 00:00:30,100
A controller circuit measures the current drop at each electrode and triangulates the exact touch coordinates.

6
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:38,937
This measurement cycle completes in roughly 10-20 milliseconds, enabling 60 Hz touch sampling rates typical of modern devices.

7
00:00:38,937 --> 00:00:51,650
Multi-touch capability, which supports pinch-to-zoom, rotation, and multi-finger gestures, is achieved by projecting a grid of drive and sense lines and scanning the capacitance changes across every intersection.

8
00:00:51,650 --> 00:01:01,775
Older resistive touchscreens rely on two flexible layers separated by a microscopic gap; applied pressure forces the layers into contact, completing a circuit.

9
00:01:01,775 --> 00:01:14,850
Resistive screens cannot detect multi-touch and are less durable, but they respond to any object (a gloved finger, a stylus) and remain common in ATMs, industrial controls, and medical devices.

10
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:26,562
The first capacitive touchscreen was demonstrated in 1965 by E. A. Johnson at the Royal Radar Establishment in Malvern, England, and described in a 1967 paper.

11
00:01:26,562 --> 00:01:35,962
Johnson's design used a single-layer capacitive surface for finger detection, decades before the technology became ubiquitous in consumer electronics.

